AU  - Chukwu, Emelda E.; Nwaokorie, Francisca O.; Coker, Akitoye O.
TI  - Role of Anaerobes as Probiotic Organisms
PY  - 2014
AB  - Probiotics are live organisms that confer health beneficial effects to the host when consumed in adequate and controlled amount. This is a growing research area and several studies have established the use of friendly anaerobes especially of the Lactobacilli and Bifidobacteria species in the treatment and management of medical conditions including gastrointestinal diseases, allergies and cancer. Nevertheless, there are still concerns over the relevance and side effects of probiotics. In addition, there is a need to evaluate these probiotic species in order to determine their efficacy and benefits in defined medical conditions. Nigeria like other sub-Saharan African countries is burdened with diarrhea, HIV/AIDS and other infectious diseases which probiotics have been shown to improve. However severe lack of probiotic use and research in Nigeria is of particular concern. Therefore this review examines the concept of probiotics, in relation to friendly anaerobes highlighting their importance in terms of therapeutic and nutritional properties and ultimately explores how the benefits of this science can be utilized by the Nigerian populace.
