PY  - 2001
AB  - Bacterial flora acquired at birth remain relatively constant throughout life. Temporary alterations of this flora using probiotic organisms may result in positive therapeutic effects. Precise mechanisms of probiotic action are just beginning to be understood. The function of competitive inhibition, while important, has been found to be augmented by effects on the intestinal and, possibly, the systemic, immune system. A few lactobacillus and bifidobacteria species, along with Saccharomyces boulardii, have been evaluated in clinical settings using appropriate methodology. These are discussed in this review.
