AU  - Dahiya, Ritu; Buttar, Harpal S.; Gupta, Suresh K.
TI  - Pathophysiology of Diabetes‐Induced Complications
PY  - 2022
AB  - The incidences of diabetes mellitus (DM) are escalating globally among all age groups and genders. DM is characterized by impaired insulin secretion or utilization, accompanied by hyperglycemia and glycosuria, as well as by a plethora of DM-induced complications such as cardiovascular diseases (e.g. heart attack and stroke), neurological disorders (e.g. cognitive impairment, dementia, and depression), nephropathy, retinopathy, obesity, and some cancers. Therefore, DM remains one of the greatest public health challenges worldwide. The health care economic burden for treating DM-induced metabolic syndrome and chronic complications is tremendously high in both developed and developing countries. A wide array of factors promote the occurrence of DM: namely, genetic and epigenetic factors, sedentary habits and lack of exercise, excessive intake of sugar and sugar-loaded drinks, unhealthy dietary habits, excessive alcohol drinking and substances of abuse, environmental pollutants, and exposure to endocrine disrupters. DM-induced hyperglycemia alters several metabolic pathways and causes pathophysiological changes in the vital organs, as well as adversely affects the quality of life in diabetic patients. Currently, insulin and oral hypoglycemic agents are used as the first-line therapies to treat DM, but these medications are very expensive and beyond the affordability range of many patients, especially in developing countries. Also, the chronic use of insulin and oral hypoglycemic agents cause unwanted side effects in diabetic patients. People are desperately looking for economically affordable alternative therapies to treat DM and its complications. Polyphenolic flavonoids present in vegetables, fruits, and medicinal plants, as well as flavonoid supplements have shown promising hypoglycemic effects, and these alternative remedies are recognized as potential cost-effective strategies for treating DM and associated complications. The oxidative stress in diabetic patients triggers excessive production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and reactive nitrogen species (RNS), such as superoxide, hydroxyl, and nitric oxide radicals that lead to the oxidation of lipids and proteins in cells and can damage the DNA. The natural antioxidants like polyphenols and carotenoids act as free radical scavengers and restore redox homoeostasis and balance between oxidation and antioxidation in the body. The unabated production of high levels of free radicals and disturbance in the redox homeostasis in DM patients not only cause detrimental changes in the cellular signaling mechanisms but also produce multiple, noncommunicable diseases. Thus, the intake of diets containing antioxidant and anti-inflammatory flavonoids and flavonoid supplements can play an important role in reducing the DM-induced hyperglycemia and associated complications. The goals of this review are to highlight the management of diabetic complications with dietary flavonoids and flavonoid supplements, as well as by lifestyle modifications (e.g. 30 minutes. exercise/day, lesser intake of sugar, or sugar free drinks). The intake of Paleolithic-diet, functional foods, bioactive ingredients of medicinal plants/herbs/spices, and probiotics can also prevent DM and the multiple disorders associated with this chronic malady. This review will provide evidence about the promising therapeutic efficacy and safety of flavonoids and flavonoid supplements from studies done in animal models and a limited number of clinical trials for the prevention and management of DM-induced complications.
